3.1 Flowable Fill Chemistry & Rheology
Formulation: Processed ash mixed with water and a low-concentration alkali activator solution (2–5% 𝑁𝑎𝑂𝐻2–%NaOH by weight).
Rheology: Engineered as a high-slump, self-leveling non-segregating grout that flows thousands of feet through standard mine boreholes and underground workings under gravity feed.
Cured Strength: Reaches 1,000 to 1,500 PSI 1,000 to 1,500PSI compressive strength in-situ—far exceeding standard civil structural fill specifications (100–300 PSI
100–300PSI) while maintaining low batching costs.
3.2 Permanent Environmental & Structural Reclamation
Subsidence Prevention (Pillar Jacketing): Abandoned room-and-pillar mines suffer progressive spalling, pillar crushing, and roof collapse over decades. The flowable fill forms a solid structural jacket around deteriorating coal pillars and supports the roof rock, eliminating surface subsidence risks for surface landowners and civil infrastructure above.
Permanent Halting of Acid Mine Drainage (AMD):
AMD is created when pyrite (𝐹𝑒𝑆2FeS 2​ ) in legacy coal seams is exposed to both oxygen and water.
Traditional treatment methods spend millions annually on continuous surface lime neutralization dosing after the acid water has already escaped.
The Solution: The flow-fill completely fills the void from floor to ceiling, entombing the exposed coal surfaces. It cuts off all atmospheric oxygen ingress and blocks groundwater circulation, permanently extinguishing the chemical reaction that generates AMD.
